// GitHub issue #533: one core at a time inside an ext2 MUTATION.
//
// kernel/fs/ext2/ext2.tkb mints directory owners from the tree.
// ext2_claim_directory says so in its own words: "minting from the tree is
// sound only while one core at a time reaches ext2 mutation: two claims of
// the same entry would be two owners of one block", and the thing it named
// as holding that was an ADMISSION rule -- every process that reaches the
// filesystem admitted to core 0 only. That is a claim about the scheduler,
// which nothing in ext2 can check, and issue #9 is in the business of
// removing it. So the claim has to become a lock.
//
// WHAT THIS EXCLUDES, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT
//
// Mutators exclude each other. Readers do NOT take this lock, and that is
// the whole design rather than an omission:
//
// - kernel/tests/common/views/peer_filesystem.expected asserts the BLOCK
//   DEVICE mutex is contended on both cpus. That verdict is produced by two
//   readers reaching the device at the same time (memory.tkb counts a wait
//   only when its trylock fails), so a lock held across reads would zero one
//   side of the very evidence issue #533 built. Readers must stay
//   concurrent.
// - Reads are also the common case by orders of magnitude. Once the boot's
//   fixtures are done, mutation is rare, and block_read's own fast paths --
//   cache hit, read-ahead run -- return before any lock at all.
//
// So a reader instead ASKS whether a mutation is in flight, with one
// acquire-load and no spin. A peer reader that finds one is sent to core 0
// by the migration gate (SyscallAction::Migrate: rerun this syscall on core
// 0). A core 0 reader has nowhere to go, so it waits for an interrupt and
// reruns (GitHub issue #597). Neither is an error returned to userspace,
// which would fail /bin/peer-read's chunk verdict.
//
// RANK 20, OUTSIDE THE DEVICE LOCK
//
// A mutator holds this across several block reads and writes, each of which
// takes memory.tkb's block_device_lock at rank 30 underneath it. The
// compiler checks that order (GitHub issue #466): acquiring a lower rank
// while holding a higher one is a type error, so this being 20 and the
// device being 30 is what makes "filesystem outside device" checked rather
// than asserted. kernel/CONCURRENCY.md's lock order records it.
//
// A TaskMutex and not a Mutex for the reason memory.tkb gives for the lock
// below this one: masking interrupts for the duration of a directory
// rewrite would bound the whole kernel's interrupt latency by it, and
// nothing reaches ext2 from an interrupt handler. `may_block` on the
// acquire makes that a compile error rather than a rule.
//
// The lock lives in THIS file rather than beside syscall_ext2_mount, on
// kernel/CONCURRENCY.md's rule that a guard type and the data it protects in
// one file leave the data reachable from the rest of that file -- and
// syscall.tkb is four thousand lines.
//
// Current limitations (2026-09-16). Keep this list current as call sites
// change; a limitation that goes stale here is worse than none, since it
// invites relying on a guarantee that quietly stopped holding. Nothing
// enforces this section's presence here: kernel/fs/ is not one of
// scripts/check_kernel_lib_limitations_header.py's directories yet.
//
// - BOTH WINDOWS ARE CLOSED FOR EVERY READER (GitHub issues #559, #597).
//   A read starting during a mutation is refused at syscall entry; a
//   mutation starting during a read waits for the reader count
//   (ext2_read_enter / ext2_read_leave) to drain. Every CPU's readers are
//   counted since mutation runs on a peer too, and an execve's registration
//   is held until its child exec prepare has streamed the image.
// - A READER OUTSIDE A SYSCALL IS NOT COUNTED. The count is taken by the
//   syscall gate (syscall_reaches_filesystem) and by nothing else, so an
//   ext2 read added on another path -- a fault handler, a kernel thread --
//   would not be. Today the only one is the boot fixture's, before any
//   peer runs a process.
// - A MUTATOR WAITING ON ITS OWN CPU'S READER WOULD WAIT FOREVER. It cannot
//   happen while KERNEL_PREEMPTIBLE is 0: a CPU's count is held only inside
//   one syscall (or an execve and its prepare), which no other syscall on
//   that CPU interleaves, and the mutating syscalls are not readers.
// - NOTHING PROVES A MUTATOR TAKES IT. The five mutating syscall arms do,
//   and the guard being linear makes a missed release a compile error on
//   every path, but a sixth arm added later would compile and simply fail to
//   exclude. The structural answer is a check that every ext2 mutator is
//   reached only under this guard.
// - THE BOOT FIXTURE MUTATES WITHOUT IT, in kernel/init/ext2_fixture.tkb.
//   That is sound for the reason the old admission claim was: it runs on
//   core 0 before any peer can reach a filesystem. It is also why the check
//   above cannot simply demand the guard at every mutator call site without
//   naming that path.
use "kernel/lib/task_mutex.tkb";
use "kernel/lib/execution_model.tkb";

// No initialisation: a global's .bss zero is already SPIN_LOCK_FREE, and
// task_mutex_init FORCE-FREES, which on two cores releases a lock the other
// core holds. scripts/check_lock_discipline.py's RULE 1 is that defect.
private let mut ext2_mutation_lock: TaskMutex;

// `[lock: addr]` names the one lock it came from, and linear, so no path out
// of a mutation -- and the mutating syscall arms have a dozen each -- can
// leave it held.
linear struct Ext2MutationGuard[lock: addr] {
    private cpu: usize;
}

// GitHub issue #559: readers inside ext2 right now. A reader adds one at
// syscall entry (ext2_read_enter) and takes it away when the syscall is
// done (ext2_read_leave); a mutator, holding the lock, waits for it to reach
// zero. So a mutation starting while a read is in flight waits for that
// read, which the entry question alone did not do. Readers stay concurrent
// with each other and with the device: they never take the lock, so
// peer_filesystem.expected's contention is untouched.
//
// Every CPU's readers since GitHub issue #597 admitted mutation on a peer.
// Before that only peers were counted, because a core 0 reader could not
// overlap a mutation that also ran only on core 0.
private let mut ext2_readers: usize;

private fn ext2_readers_address() -> usize {
    return (&ext2_readers) as usize;
}

fn ext2_mutation_take() -> Ext2MutationGuard[ext2_mutation_lock]
        !{acquires_lock_20_ext2_mutation, lock_guard_20_ext2_mutation,
          unsafe} {
    static_assert(KERNEL_PREEMPTIBLE == 0,
                  "a mutator waits for every CPU's ext2 readers, so a reader preempted on this CPU would be waited for forever");
    task_mutex_acquire(&ext2_mutation_lock);
    // The Dekker half: the lock is visibly held before the count is read,
    // and ext2_read_enter publishes its count before it reads the lock, so
    // at least one side sees the other. A reader that saw the lock free is
    // counted here and waited for; one that saw it held has undone its
    // count and gone to core 0.
    dsb_ish();
    while (unsafe { atomic_load_acquire(ext2_readers_address()) } != 0) { }
    let mut guard: Ext2MutationGuard[ext2_mutation_lock] = { cpu_id() };
    return guard;
}

// For kernel/kernel/ext2_mutation_contention_evidence.tkb's control phase
// only: the lock without the wait, which is what ext2_mutation_take was
// before #559. `unsafe` so any other use is visible in the trusted base.
fn ext2_mutation_take_without_readers_unchecked()
        -> Ext2MutationGuard[ext2_mutation_lock]
        !{acquires_lock_20_ext2_mutation, lock_guard_20_ext2_mutation,
          unsafe} {
    task_mutex_acquire(&ext2_mutation_lock);
    let mut guard: Ext2MutationGuard[ext2_mutation_lock] = { cpu_id() };
    return guard;
}

// A reader's arrival. True: counted and admitted, and ext2_read_leave is
// owed. False: a mutation is in flight, nothing is owed, and the caller
// reruns the syscall -- on core 0 from a peer, after an interrupt on core 0.
fn ext2_read_enter() -> bool !{unsafe} {
    unsafe { atomic_fetch_add_relaxed(ext2_readers_address(), 1); }
    dsb_ish();
    if (task_mutex_is_held(&ext2_mutation_lock)) {
        unsafe {
            atomic_fetch_add_relaxed(ext2_readers_address(),
                                     0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF);
        }
        return false;
    }
    return true;
}

// Every ext2 access of the syscall is complete before the count drops.
fn ext2_read_leave() !{unsafe} {
    dsb_ish();
    unsafe {
        atomic_fetch_add_relaxed(ext2_readers_address(),
                                 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF);
    }
}

fn ext2_mutation_give(guard: sink Ext2MutationGuard[ext2_mutation_lock]) {
    task_mutex_release(&ext2_mutation_lock);
}

// The reader's question. One acquire-load of the lock word, no spin, no
// wait: true means some core is inside a mutation right now.
fn ext2_mutation_in_flight() -> bool !{unsafe} {
    return task_mutex_is_held(&ext2_mutation_lock);
}
